items.rootCause:
What the APK 20 slices show. I clustered every createViewUnsafe in the six atrace files by time and type. The counts are identical in all six runs, sound ON and OFF.

(1) Cause common to both conditions: the hit's own commit creates 141 native views (+0.40 to +0.89 s after the marker): 94 RNSVGPath, 21 RNSVGCircle, 6 RNSVGGroup, 3 RNSVGSvgView, 1 ClipPath, 1 Defs, 1 Ellipse, 2 Rect, 8 RCTView, 3 RCTText and 1 AndroidTextInput.
- There is exactly one ClipPath and one Defs. That means one whole avatar picture is drawn from scratch. The only avatar that mounts fresh at a hit is the sender's face in the hit banner. The banner (HitBanner) was not mounted between hits.
- The banner accounts for about 130 of the 141 views: that avatar (~120 SVG nodes plus its 3 frame views), the ring's Svg with 2 circles, the ring's number (an animated read-only TextInput, an EditText that takes 8–18 ms to create on its own), and its texts and views.
- This is the 165–200 ms JS burst before the mount, the 3–5 mount frames (preMountItems 14–30 ms each), and the 21–33 ms first Record View#draw (react-native-svg draws in software). All of it falls in the frames of the fade-in.
- On the first hit, the JS side also pays a one-time cost: building and parsing the markup of the sender's face for the first time (staticXml/svgAst, cached afterwards). That fits 'the second warp is smoother'.

(2) A second mount 250–500 ms after the hit: 68 views (1 SvgView, 26 paths, 36 rects, no defs). This is my own card's avatar going into its moving 'dizzy + wobble' layers (AVATAR_REACTIONS.on.hit). In on2 it lines up with the 110 ms stall, together with the GC.

(3) Every warp, not only the first: during the effect, the main thread itself draws the Skia overlay with GL. In on1 around +0.88 s the main thread shows acquireBuffer, then about 20 ms of untraced CPU, then dequeueBuffer, eglSwapBuffers and queueBuffer on SurfaceTexture-0, and waits on Mali fences. That is the 25–47 ms 'animation' phase, and it holds the effect at about 30 fps.

(4) Sound ON only: CPU contention, nothing blocking. ExoPlayer rebuilds its AudioTrack at every play: the new tids are only ExoPlayer:Audio and AudioTrack. The Playback/Loader/first-AudioTrack tids 7860–7862 are the already warmed player, waking up. doSomeWork takes 82–124 ms of wall time but only 36–45 ms of CPU. A NativeAlloc concurrent GC followed in 2 of 3 runs.

Ruled out by the traces:
- a blocking GC on main;
- shader or GL compile (≤10 ms);
- a long binder call;
- D state;
- audio player creation on main;
- the volume setter.

items.test:
apps/mobile/src/screens/game/hit/__tests__/bannerStandby.test.tsx, now 10 tests. HitBanner and Avatar are wrapped to count mounts and renders.

- (a) The order of the constants.
- (b) Changed to check the review's main point:
  - nothing before 2.25 s;
  - then the bare banner only (ring number present, 0 avatars, avatarMounts {});
  - nothing at gap minus 1 ms;
  - then Nina's avatar alone, then Omer's;
  - unseen, accessible=false, no-hide-descendants;
  - ticks and an equal list do not re-render it;
  - Omer's warp mounts no banner or avatar, and the banner is back in standby afterwards.
- (c) A sender who is not in the list gets an avatar at the hit.
- (d) A hit before the standby makes its own banner, and the standby comes after the hit.
- (e) New, for the mid-hit change. Profiles change during a hit: no avatar is mounted under the hit, or in the hit's last commit. Omer's avatar comes on the next idle moment after the hit. A sender who left is dropped, and the banner is not remounted.
- (f) New, for the early end. The hit's clock is held at 2500 ms (makeMutable passed as `elapsed`) and the hit is cleared. The standby's visible opacity (animated opacity times its ancestors' opacity) is 0. The control check in the same test: the shown banner is at 1.
- (g) New, for the rehearsal. HitRehearsal with banner={false} never mounts hit-rehearsal-banner.
- (h, i) hitSendersOf.

Failing on the old code:
- I removed the slot's opacity and the busy hold from HitLayer for a moment. Tests (e) and (f) failed (2 failed), and the file was restored afterwards.
- Test (b)'s bare-banner checks fail on the first implementation, which mounted every avatar in the standby commit.
- Test (g) fails without the new prop.

GameScreenRenders (3 players): the commit bound goes from 24 to 26, for one commit per opponent avatar, once per game, with a comment.

Green:
- src/screens/game plus src/components/interference: 70 suites, 1387 tests (with the new bound);
- src/__tests__ plus src/testing: 65 suites, 1273 tests;
- GameScreenRenders, HitRehearsal and warpStart: 21 tests;
- all 37 suites that read motion.md: 732 tests;
- npx tsc -p apps/mobile --noEmit: 0;
- eslint on the changed files: 0.
Prettier was applied. Every changed file is LF with no BOM.

